LECT * FROM orders o JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id WHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\n1. **`lower(u.email)` in the WHERE clause (high impact).** Wrapping the column in a function stops a plain index on `users.email` from being used, so the database scans the whole `users` table. Pick one of these fixes:\n   - Create an expression index: `CREATE INDEX idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));`. The query then works as written.\n   - Use a case-insensitive type or collation, such as `citext` in Postgres, and compare `u.email = 'a@example.org'` directly.\n   - Store emails already normalized to lowercase, with a unique index. Then compare `u.email = lower('a@example.org')`, which applies the function to the constant instead of the column.\n\n   An expression index on `lower(email)` can also enforce uniqueness, if you don't have that already.\n\n2. **Possibly no index on `orders.user_id` (medium impact).** Once the user lookup is fast, the join depends on finding that user's orders quickly. Without an index on `orders(user_id)`, every lookup scans the whole `orders` table. Foreign keys aren't indexed automatically in Postgres, and the same applies in some other databases. Check with `\\d orders` or your database's equivalent.\n\n3. **`SELECT *` (low to medium impact).** It returns every column of both tables, including `u.id` and `o.user_id` twice. That means more I/O and network traffic. It also rules out index-only scans, and it breaks clients that read columns by name. List only the columns you need.\n\nHere is a version with those changes:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n-- needs: INDEX users (lower(email)), INDEX orders (user_id)\n```\n\nI only had the query text, not the schema, indexes, or row counts. The column names in the rewrite are placeholders. To confirm the fixes, run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after, and look for a sequential scan on `users` turning into an index scan.\n\nThe `sql-database-assistant` skill loaded with no instructions of its own, so this review is based only on the query.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}